  "worked_solution": "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-12x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-12x+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-4x+4) & =0 \\\\ 3(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=2$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(0) & =3(0)^{2}-12(0)+12=12>0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(3) & =3(3)^{2}-12(3)+12=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{\u2032}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $\u2197$ |  | $\u2197$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=2.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=2$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
